"summary", "oldValue": "César Gabriel de Choiseul, Duke of Praslin was a French officer, diplomat and statesman.", "newValue": "César Gabriel de Choiseul, Duke of Praslin, emerged as a pivotal French statesman and diplomat during the tumultuous mid-18th century, a period defined by the global rivalries of the Seven Years' War and its aftermath. Born in 1712, he rose through the ranks of the French military and diplomatic corps, eventually serving as Minister of Foreign Affairs (1761–1766) and Minister of Marine (1766–1770) under King Louis XV. His career was inextricably linked with the efforts to restore French prestige and power following significant territorial and colonial losses, making him a central figure in the re-evaluation of Bourbon foreign policy.

His most defining contribution was his instrumental role in negotiating the Treaty of Paris in 1763, which formally concluded the Seven Years' War. While France ceded vast territories in North America (Canada) and India to Great Britain, Choiseul skillfully preserved French sugar islands in the Caribbean, such as Martinique and Guadeloupe, which were vital for the French economy. As Minister of Marine, he embarked on an ambitious program to rebuild and modernize the French Royal Navy, investing heavily in shipbuilding and naval infrastructure, an effort aimed at challenging British maritime supremacy in the future.

Choiseul's diplomatic acumen and administrative reforms had a lasting impact on France's strategic positioning in the decades leading up to the French Revolution. His efforts to strengthen the navy laid foundational groundwork, albeit incomplete, for France's later involvement in the American Revolutionary War. He also contributed to the consolidation of the Choiseul family's influence in French politics, working in tandem with his powerful cousin, Étienne François, Duc de Choiseul, to steer national policy and maintain a semblance of stability in a volatile political climate."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-19T23:09:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44634892673", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Early Modern era: age of exploration, printing revolution, and religious reformation", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1712-08-15, Paris"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "1712-08-15, Paris"}], "newValue": ["The devastating losses suffered by France during the Seven Years' War created an urgent need for skilled diplomatic leadership to negotiate peace.", "The intricate web of 18th-century European alliances and rivalries necessitated experienced statesmen to navigate international relations.", "César Gabriel de Choiseul's aristocratic lineage and familial connections, particularly to his cousin Étienne François, Duc de Choiseul, facilitated his ascent in French government."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-19T23:09:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44634892673", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped political institutions and governance practices", "type": "Idea", "year": "1785-11-15"}, {"title": "Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution", "type": "Movement", "year": "1785-11-15"}], "newValue": ["The Treaty of Paris (1763) redefined the global colonial landscape, with France ceding significant territo